{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbK8ALdCmzp7LYC6X8zu", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "`git rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> [<branch>]` replays the commits that are in `<branch>` but not in `<upstream>` on top of `<newbase>`. A plain `git rebase` fixes the base to the upstream. `--onto` lets you pick the new base and the cut point separately.\n\n**Example:** you branched `feature` off `topic`, but you want `feature` to sit on `main` without dragging along `topic`'s commits.\n\n```\nBefore:\nmain:     A---B\n               \\\ntopic:          C---D\n                     \\\nfeature:              E---F\n```\n\n```\ngit rebase --onto main topic feature\n```\n\n- `main` is the new base.\n- `topic` is the old base. Commits reachable from `feature` but not from `topic` (E and F) are the ones that get moved.\n- `feature` is the branch to rebase. If you leave it out, the current branch is used.\n\n```\nAfter:\nmain:     A---B\n              |\\\n              | E'---F'    (feature)\n              \\\ntopic:          C---D\n```\n\nE and F are recreated as E' and F' (new hashes) on top of B. `topic` is untouched.\n\n**Other common uses:**\n- Drop a range of commits: `git rebase --onto A B` removes the commits after `A` up to and including `B`.\n- Move a branch after its parent was squash-merged: `git rebase --onto main <old-parent> feature`.\n\nBecause it rewrites commits, avoid using it on branches others have already pulled. If you've pushed, you'll need `--force-with-lease`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 6685, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 6685}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "c